server: Keep the permissions given to files and folders.

A security descriptor set on a file or folder (SetNamedSecurityInfo,
NtSetSecurityObject) was only turned into the Unix mode, and the next open
built one again from that mode: whoever had been given access was gone. The
Epic Games Launcher prepares a game's folder by granting the players' group
full access from a second copy of itself, then reads the folder's permissions
back and looks for that grant; it never found it and stopped the install with
error DP-07 ("Authorize success without gaining access").

The descriptor set last is now kept with the file in an extended attribute
(user.wine.sd), together with the Unix mode it was set with, and is what the
file reports from then on. The mode is still set as before. When the mode has
since been changed outside Wine, the stored descriptor no longer describes
the file and the one built from the mode is reported again. Access to the
file when it is opened is decided as before, from the Unix mode.

Generic rights in what is set (GENERIC_ALL) are mapped to the file's own, as
Windows does, and programs read back the mapped rights.
Entries that only pass to what is created inside keep them.

